    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 18-year-old patient presents with chronic, dull knee pain. Radiographs reveal a well-demarcated, small lytic lesion exclusively located within the epiphysis of the proximal tibia. Biopsy shows ''chicken-wire'' calcifications and mononuclear cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Chondromyxoid fibroma", "Chondroblastoma", "Giant Cell Tumor", "Osteosarcoma", "Enchondroma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Chondroblastoma is a rare, benign cartilage-forming tumor that characteristically occurs in the epiphysis (or apophysis) of long bones in skeletally immature patients (unlike Giant Cell Tumors, which usually occur after physeal closure). Histology classically shows ''''chicken-wire'''' calcifications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old presents with a painless, hard bump near the knee. Radiographs show a bony outgrowth from the distal femoral metaphysis that points away from the joint. The medullary cavity of the lesion is continuous with the medullary cavity of the native bone. What is the most feared complication if this patient has multiple similar lesions?', '["Malignant transformation to secondary chondrosarcoma", "Malignant transformation to osteosarcoma", "Pathologic fracture through the stalk", "Development of multiple myeloma", "Systemic metastasis to the lungs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is an osteochondroma. While solitary osteochondromas have a <1% risk of malignant transformation, patients with Multiple Hereditary Exostoses (EXT1/EXT2 mutations) have a higher risk (up to 5-10%) of a lesion degenerating into a secondary chondrosarcoma. A rapidly growing or painful cartilage cap (> 2 cm in an adult) is a warning sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal humerus. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Surgical resection alone", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal tibia. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Radiation therapy", "Amputation", "Denosumab", "Bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 18-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal tibia. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Bisphosphonates", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Radiation therapy", "Amputation", "Denosumab"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 27-year-old patient presents with a swollen, deformed hand. Radiographs show multiple centrally located radiolucent lesions with stippled calcifications within the phalanges and metacarpals. Physical exam reveals multiple soft tissue hemangiomas on the same limb. What is the diagnosis?', '["Multiple hereditary exostoses", "Neurofibromatosis type 1", "Maffucci syndrome", "McCune-Albright syndrome", "Ollier disease"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors of the medullary cavity, frequently found in the small bones of the hands and feet. Multiple enchondromas alone characterize Ollier disease. When multiple enchondromas are accompanied by soft tissue hemangiomas, it is called Maffucci syndrome, which carries a much higher risk of malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma and other visceral malignancies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old patient requires an open incisional biopsy for a suspected high-grade soft tissue sarcoma of the anterior thigh. Which of the following is a critical principle when performing this biopsy?', '["Obtain a small sample of the necrotic center rather than the periphery to ensure a diagnosis", "Use a transverse incision parallel to skin tension lines for a better cosmetic result", "Dissect extensively through intermuscular planes to avoid muscle damage", "Exsanguinate the limb with an Esmarch bandage before inflating the tourniquet", "Use a longitudinal incision placed within the planned definitive resection ellipse"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Orthopedic oncology biopsy principles: 1) Longitudinal incisions (transverse incisions contaminate more tissue longitudinally). 2) The biopsy tract must be excisable en bloc with the tumor. 3) Avoid spreading through fascial planes (split muscle instead). 4) Do NOT exsanguinate with an Esmarch to prevent pushing tumor cells centrally. 5) Biopsy the periphery (the advancing edge), as the center is often necrotic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old child presents with a limp. Radiographs of the proximal femur show a ''shepherd''''s crook'' deformity with an intramedullary lesion demonstrating a ''ground-glass'' appearance. The patient also has large, irregular cafe-au-lait spots with ''coast of Maine'' borders. What other clinical feature might this patient exhibit?', '["Multiple lipomas", "Precocious puberty", "Hearing loss and cranial nerve palsies", "Axillary freckling and Lisch nodules", "Retinal hemangioblastomas"]'::jsonb, 1, 'McCune-Albright syndrome consists of the triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia (ground-glass bone lesions, shepherd''''s crook deformity), café-au-lait macules with irregular borders (coast of Maine), and endocrine abnormalities (most commonly precocious puberty). It is caused by an activating mutation in the GNAS gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal femur. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Radiation therapy", "Denosumab", "Amputation", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 8-year-old child sustains a minor ankle sprain. Radiographs are negative for fracture but show an incidental, 2 cm, eccentric, bubbly, cortically based radiolucent lesion with a thin sclerotic margin in the distal tibial metaphysis. The patient has no pain directly over the lesion. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Curettage and bone grafting", "Reassurance and observation", "Prophylactic internal fixation", "MRI of the entire tibia", "Immediate core needle biopsy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This classic description is a Non-Ossifying Fibroma (NOF) or Fibrous Cortical Defect. These are asymptomatic, developmental defects seen in up to 30-40% of normal children. They invariably resolve/ossify spontaneously over time and require only observation unless they are large enough (>50% of bone diameter) to pose a risk of pathologic fracture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 16-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the proximal femur that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Wide surgical resection", "Curettage and bone grafting", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Radiation therapy", "Chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old presents with a painless, hard bump near the knee. Radiographs show a bony outgrowth from the distal femoral metaphysis that points away from the joint. The medullary cavity of the lesion is continuous with the medullary cavity of the native bone. What is the most feared complication if this patient has multiple similar lesions?', '["Systemic metastasis to the lungs", "Malignant transformation to osteosarcoma", "Pathologic fracture through the stalk", "Development of multiple myeloma", "Malignant transformation to secondary chondrosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is an osteochondroma. While solitary osteochondromas have a <1% risk of malignant transformation, patients with Multiple Hereditary Exostoses (EXT1/EXT2 mutations) have a higher risk (up to 5-10%) of a lesion degenerating into a secondary chondrosarcoma. A rapidly growing or painful cartilage cap (> 2 cm in an adult) is a warning sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the tibia that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Cryoablation without imaging", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Radiation therapy", "Open en bloc resection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the pelvis. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts."]'::jsonb, 4, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 21-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the humerus that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open en bloc resection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Radiation therapy", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Cryoablation without imaging"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the pelvis. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["Wide surgical resection", "Radiofrequency ablation", "Systemic chemotherapy", "High-dose radiation therapy", "Denosumab therapy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 59-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the radius. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 51-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the radius. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Osteoblast overactivation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old female presents with pain and swelling in the distal femur. Imaging shows an eccentric, expansile, lytic metaphyseal lesion. An MRI reveals multiple fluid-fluid levels within the lesion. Histology shows blood-filled spaces lacking an endothelial lining. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Telangiectatic Osteosarcoma", "Chondroblastoma", "Giant Cell Tumor", "Aneurysmal Bone Cyst (ABC)", "Unicameral Bone Cyst (UBC)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Aneurysmal bone cysts (ABCs) are expansile, blood-filled cystic lesions. The classic MRI finding is ''''fluid-fluid levels'''' representing separation of serum and red blood cells. Treatment often involves extended curettage, high-speed burring, and bone grafting.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 29-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the femur that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Radiation therapy", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Open en bloc resection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 21-year-old patient presents with a swollen, deformed hand. Radiographs show multiple centrally located radiolucent lesions with stippled calcifications within the phalanges and metacarpals. Physical exam reveals multiple soft tissue hemangiomas on the same limb. What is the diagnosis?', '["Neurofibromatosis type 1", "Multiple hereditary exostoses", "McCune-Albright syndrome", "Ollier disease", "Maffucci syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors of the medullary cavity, frequently found in the small bones of the hands and feet. Multiple enchondromas alone characterize Ollier disease. When multiple enchondromas are accompanied by soft tissue hemangiomas, it is called Maffucci syndrome, which carries a much higher risk of malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma and other visceral malignancies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the humerus that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open en bloc resection", "Radiation therapy", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Cryoablation without imaging"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old patient presents with knee pain. Radiographs reveal an eccentric, expansile, lytic lesion resembling a ''soap bubble'' in the epiphysis of the distal femur extending to the subchondral bone. Biopsy shows mononuclear cells and numerous multinucleated giant cells. Which targeted systemic therapy can be used as an adjunct for large, unresectable, or recurrent lesions?', '["Denosumab (RANKL inhibitor)", "Methotrexate", "Infliximab (TNF-alpha inhibitor)", "Imatinib (Tyrosine kinase inhibitor)", "Rituximab (Anti-CD20)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Giant Cell Tumor of bone classically occurs in skeletally mature individuals (epiphysis/metaphysis). The neoplastic mononuclear stromal cells express RANKL, which recruits and activates the multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells. Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, is an effective targeted therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male presents with severe back pain. Radiographs of the lumbar spine show a dense, sclerotic (osteoblastic) lesion in the L3 vertebral body. Laboratory studies show an elevated alkaline phosphatase, but normal calcium. Which primary malignancy is most likely responsible for this metastatic lesion?', '["Prostate carcinoma", "Malignant melanoma", "Thyroid carcinoma", "Multiple myeloma", "Renal cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Metastatic prostate cancer characteristically produces osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone lesions due to the secretion of factors that stimulate bone formation (like BMPs). Conversely, cancers like renal cell, thyroid, and multiple myeloma typically cause osteolytic (destructive) lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the humerus. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 22-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal pelvis. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Bisphosphonates", "Amputation", "Radiation therapy", "Denosumab"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the femur. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Osteoblast overactivation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the humerus. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Necrotic tumor debris", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 51-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal tibia. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 22-year-old patient presents with a swollen, deformed hand. Radiographs show multiple centrally located radiolucent lesions with stippled calcifications within the phalanges and metacarpals. Physical exam reveals multiple soft tissue hemangiomas on the same limb. What is the diagnosis?', '["Neurofibromatosis type 1", "McCune-Albright syndrome", "Ollier disease", "Maffucci syndrome", "Multiple hereditary exostoses"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors of the medullary cavity, frequently found in the small bones of the hands and feet. Multiple enchondromas alone characterize Ollier disease. When multiple enchondromas are accompanied by soft tissue hemangiomas, it is called Maffucci syndrome, which carries a much higher risk of malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma and other visceral malignancies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 23-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the tibia that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open en bloc resection", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Radiation therapy", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 11-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the pelvis that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Radiation therapy", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Open en bloc resection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-year-old patient presents with knee pain. Radiographs reveal an eccentric, expansile, lytic lesion resembling a ''soap bubble'' in the epiphysis of the distal femur extending to the subchondral bone. Biopsy shows mononuclear cells and numerous multinucleated giant cells. Which targeted systemic therapy can be used as an adjunct for large, unresectable, or recurrent lesions?', '["Methotrexate", "Denosumab (RANKL inhibitor)", "Infliximab (TNF-alpha inhibitor)", "Rituximab (Anti-CD20)", "Imatinib (Tyrosine kinase inhibitor)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Giant Cell Tumor of bone classically occurs in skeletally mature individuals (epiphysis/metaphysis). The neoplastic mononuclear stromal cells express RANKL, which recruits and activates the multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells. Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, is an effective targeted therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old patient presents with back pain and fatigue. Radiographs of the skull and spine show multiple ''punched-out'' radiolucencies without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. Laboratory tests reveal hypercalcemia, anemia, and an M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. Which bone scan finding is expected?', '["Increased uptake exclusively in the appendicular skeleton", "Diffuse periosteal uptake", "Intensely increased uptake (''hot'' spots) in all lesions", "A falsely negative (''cold'' or normal) bone scan", "A ''superscan'' with absent renal visualization"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Multiple myeloma lesions are purely osteolytic, caused by myeloma cells stimulating osteoclasts (via RANKL) and actively inhibiting osteoblasts (via DKK-1). Because a Technetium-99m bone scan relies on osteoblastic activity (bone formation), it is notoriously unreliable (falsely negative) in multiple myeloma. A skeletal survey (plain films) or low-dose whole-body CT is the preferred imaging modality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal radius. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 59-year-old patient requires an open incisional biopsy for a suspected high-grade soft tissue sarcoma of the anterior thigh. Which of the following is a critical principle when performing this biopsy?', '["Use a transverse incision parallel to skin tension lines for a better cosmetic result", "Exsanguinate the limb with an Esmarch bandage before inflating the tourniquet", "Obtain a small sample of the necrotic center rather than the periphery to ensure a diagnosis", "Use a longitudinal incision placed within the planned definitive resection ellipse", "Dissect extensively through intermuscular planes to avoid muscle damage"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Orthopedic oncology biopsy principles: 1) Longitudinal incisions (transverse incisions contaminate more tissue longitudinally). 2) The biopsy tract must be excisable en bloc with the tumor. 3) Avoid spreading through fascial planes (split muscle instead). 4) Do NOT exsanguinate with an Esmarch to prevent pushing tumor cells centrally. 5) Biopsy the periphery (the advancing edge), as the center is often necrotic.'),
