    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal femur. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the radius. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Necrotic tumor debris", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Pus settling from an abscess"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 33-year-old patient presents with a slowly growing, deep, painless mass in the popliteal fossa. MRI shows a heterogeneous mass near the knee joint, but not within the joint capsule. Biopsy confirms a soft tissue sarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a t(X;18) chromosomal translocation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Clear cell sarcoma", "Liposarcoma", "Synovial Sarcoma", "Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma", "Rhabdomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Despite its name, Synovial Sarcoma rarely arises from the actual synovium of the joint, but typically occurs near large joints (e.g., the knee) in young adults. It is strongly associated with the t(X;18) translocation, which creates the SS18-SSX fusion gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old male presents with severe back pain. Radiographs of the lumbar spine show a dense, sclerotic (osteoblastic) lesion in the L3 vertebral body. Laboratory studies show an elevated alkaline phosphatase, but normal calcium. Which primary malignancy is most likely responsible for this metastatic lesion?', '["Malignant melanoma", "Renal cell carcinoma", "Thyroid carcinoma", "Prostate carcinoma", "Multiple myeloma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Metastatic prostate cancer characteristically produces osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone lesions due to the secretion of factors that stimulate bone formation (like BMPs). Conversely, cancers like renal cell, thyroid, and multiple myeloma typically cause osteolytic (destructive) lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal humerus. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 24-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the tibial diaphysis that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Radiation therapy", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Curettage and bone grafting", "Intravenous bisphosphonates", "Chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the radius that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Intra-articular steroid injection", "Radiation therapy", "Cryoablation without imaging", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Open en bloc resection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal tibia. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 31-year-old patient presents with knee pain. Radiographs reveal an eccentric, expansile, lytic lesion resembling a ''soap bubble'' in the epiphysis of the distal femur extending to the subchondral bone. Biopsy shows mononuclear cells and numerous multinucleated giant cells. Which targeted systemic therapy can be used as an adjunct for large, unresectable, or recurrent lesions?', '["Rituximab (Anti-CD20)", "Methotrexate", "Denosumab (RANKL inhibitor)", "Imatinib (Tyrosine kinase inhibitor)", "Infliximab (TNF-alpha inhibitor)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Giant Cell Tumor of bone classically occurs in skeletally mature individuals (epiphysis/metaphysis). The neoplastic mononuclear stromal cells express RANKL, which recruits and activates the multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells. Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, is an effective targeted therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 21-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal pelvis. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Amputation", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Bisphosphonates", "Radiation therapy", "Denosumab"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 51-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the humerus. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the proximal femur. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["Systemic chemotherapy", "High-dose radiation therapy", "Denosumab therapy", "Radiofrequency ablation", "Wide surgical resection"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the proximal femur. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["High-dose radiation therapy", "Denosumab therapy", "Wide surgical resection", "Systemic chemotherapy", "Radiofrequency ablation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.');
